     1	# Lifecycle Predicate Unification — Design Spec
     2
     3	Date: 2026-06-27
     4	Owner: Ronaldo (decisions) + agent (drafting)
     5	Status: Re-review v2 FAIL addressed in draft — pending cross-model re-review before implementation plan
     6	Supersedes: `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-06-26-stability-spacing-effect-design.md`
     7
     8	## Summary
     9
    10	Engram has **multiple writers** of `memories.lifecycle_state`. Cross-model review
    11	found **four MCP-facing decay-policy/compression tools plus one optional server
    12	compression scheduler path**, each currently capable of applying a divergent
    13	lifecycle transition. In production, lifecycle-derived state has not transitioned
    14	(1,179 memories: `archived=0`, `stale=0`) because the lifecycle tool is manual and
    15	the compression scheduler defaults to disabled. Several other writers are
    16	legitimate *domain* writers (retention max-count, conflict resolution,
    17	consolidation, dream-approved actions, manual) that act on `lifecycle_state` for
    18	non-decay reasons and must coexist. This spec unifies **decay/compression-derived
    19	lifecycle transitions** into one canonical predicate and one lifecycle-state
    20	writer for decay. It uses a hybrid temporal model (recency-primary + absolute-idle
    21	terminal cap) with importance as a continuous resistance modulator (not a binary
    22	gate). It does NOT touch legitimate domain writers except to draw the boundary
    23	explicitly.
    24
    25	It is scoped to **fix what happens when lifecycle runs**, not to add a lifecycle
    26	scheduler (that is a deferred follow-up). The preexisting optional compression
    27	scheduler is not a lifecycle scheduler after this spec: it may remain as
    28	compression-only work, but it must not turn active/stale memories into archived
    29	ones. The `stability`/spacing-effect feature is **not** in this spec — it returns
    30	as a follow-up that adds a recency multiplier on top of this corrected foundation.
    31
    32	## Problem (verified against code + production)
    33
    34	### The writer ecosystem (full enumeration, verified)
    35
    36	A `grep` for every `lifecycle_state` write across `src/` reveals **more than two**
    37	writers. They split into two classes:
    38
    39	**Decay-policy/compression paths — divergent predicates, MUST converge (this spec):**
    40
    41	| Path | Predicate | Temporal var | Site |
    42	|---|---|---|---|
    43	| `lifecycle_run` MCP tool | `importance < X AND age AND access_count < 5` | `created_at` | `lifecycle.rs:178,184` |
    44	| `run_salience_decay` MCP tool | `score < 0.2 AND days_idle >= 90` | `last_accessed_at` | `salience.rs:439-460` |
    45	| `memory_decay` MCP tool (policy) | `Active AND new_retention < 0.25 → Stale` | retention score | `memory_policy.rs:352-354` |
    46	| `memory_archive_old` MCP tool | `created_at < cutoff AND importance <= max AND access_count < min` | `created_at` | `summarize.rs:231-269,329` |
    47	| `compress_old_memories` retention/scheduler path | `created_at < cutoff AND importance <= max AND access_count < min AND active → archive original` | `created_at` | `retention.rs:157,217-318`; `server.rs:122-136,726-749` |
    48
    49	Four paths are **manual MCP tools**. The fifth path is the optional server
    50	compression scheduler (`ENGRAM_COMPRESSION_INTERVAL`, default `0`) and the
    51	explicit retention-policy compression call (`compress_after_days`). It calls
    52	`compress_old_memories`, creates a summary, and currently archives the original by
    53	setting `lifecycle_state='archived'`. The prior "manual-only" framing was false:
    54	there is no lifecycle scheduler, but there is an optional compression scheduler
    55	that currently doubles as a lifecycle writer and therefore must converge too. The
    56	original "two writers" framing missed `memory_decay`; the second framing missed
    57	`memory_archive_old`; the third missed the scheduled `compress_old_memories` entry
    58	point. All misses are part of the audit trail.
    59
    60	**Domain writers — legitimate non-decay reasons, COEXIST (out of scope):**
    61
    62	| Writer | Reason | Site |
    63	|---|---|---|
    64	| `consolidation_offline` | archives on consolidation/supersession | `consolidation_offline.rs:568` |
    65	| `retention_policy_apply` max-count | explicit workspace cap policy | `retention.rs:182` |
    66	| `retention_policy_apply` auto-delete | explicit retention soft-delete of already-archived rows | `retention.rs:204` |
    67	| `context_quality::resolve_conflict` | explicit conflict resolution (`KeepA`/`KeepB`) | `context_quality.rs:730,737` |
    68	| `dream` candidate apply | reviewed/approved action | `dream.rs:377` |
    69	| `memory_set_lifecycle` | manual explicit | `lifecycle.rs:239` |
    70
    71	These are **not** concurrent decay engines. They are deliberate, non-decay
    72	lifecycle/visibility mutations (auto-delete writes `valid_to`, not
    73	`lifecycle_state`) and are explicitly preserved.
    74
    75	Additional write sites are helpers/initializers/tests, not lifecycle engines:
    76	`storage/queries/lifecycle.rs:28,39` is the query-layer helper whose callers
    77	decide domain/engine semantics; `turso_backend.rs:651-675` and
    78	`migrations.rs:926` initialize/default `active`; handler/storage tests set up
    79	fixtures.
    80
    81	`retention_policy_apply` compression is intentionally absent from the preserved
    82	writer list while `compress_old_memories` writes `lifecycle_state`: that behavior
    83	is in the convergence scope above. After implementation, retention compression may
    84	remain as a domain operation only if it compresses/summarizes rows that are already
    85	`Archived` and does not decide that a memory becomes archived.
    86
    87	> **Note (`memory_policy.rs:146`, `summarize.rs:329`, `retention.rs:312`):**
    88	> `memory_decay`, `memory_archive_old`, and `compress_old_memories` currently write
    89	> lifecycle state outside the canonical lifecycle decision. This spec's decisions
    90	> for them (below) supersede those writes regardless of whether the caller is an
    91	> MCP tool, an explicit retention policy apply, or the optional server scheduler.
    92
    93	#### Decision for the decay/compression paths
    94
    95	- `lifecycle_run` → becomes the **single canonical decay writer** (uses
    96	  `decide_lifecycle_state`).
    97	- `run_salience_decay` → **disarmed**, score-only (remove `salience.rs:439-460`).
    98	- `memory_decay` (policy) → **disarmed** of its `lifecycle_target` transition
    99	  (`memory_policy.rs:352-357` returns `None` for lifecycle; it keeps writing
   100	  policy *scores* — retention/priority — but no longer transitions
   101	  `lifecycle_state`). Rationale: its retention-score predicate is another
   102	  divergent model; consolidating it into `decide_lifecycle_state` is the same
   103	  unification. If a retention-score signal should influence decay, that is a
   104	  mandatory follow-up that feeds the canonical predicate, not a parallel writer.
   105	- `memory_archive_old` → **disarmed** of lifecycle transitions in this spec. Its
   106	  current age/importance/access-count predicate is the same class of decay
   107	  predicate being removed from `lifecycle_run`. To preserve the single-writer
   108	  invariant, it must stop setting `lifecycle_state`; compression/summarization can
   109	  continue only for rows already `Archived` by the canonical lifecycle path, or
   110	  move to a follow-up that explicitly redesigns the tool as
   111	  `compress_archived_memories`.
   112	- `compress_old_memories` (retention compression + optional server scheduler) →
   113	  **disarmed** of lifecycle transitions in this spec. This is the concrete
   114	  resolution of the re-review v2 blocker. The function may create summaries only
   115	  for rows already `Archived`, or it may be split/renamed in a follow-up, but it
   116	  must not change `Active`/`Stale` rows to `Archived`. This applies equally to
   117	  `retention_policy_apply` (`compress_after_days`) and to the background
   118	  compression scheduler enabled by `ENGRAM_COMPRESSION_INTERVAL`. Any log/result
   119	  wording that currently says "archived" must become "compressed" or equivalent.
   120
   121	### Why the salience predicate is structurally inert
   122
   123	Verified in `salience.rs:254-278`: archive requires `score < 0.2 AND
   124	days_inactive >= 90` (ANDed). A default memory (importance=0.5, feedback default
   125	0.5 at `salience.rs:293`) scores ~0.27 from importance+feedback alone, permanently
   126	above 0.2. So the score gate is dead for typical memories; only the day-count
   127	gate could ever drive archival — and stability (as the old spec specced) only
   128	moved the recency component (weight 0.30), giving near-zero archival effect. The
   129	old stability spec was built on this inert predicate; its review rounds (1-2)
   130	added real rigor (the importance floor, the AND/OR asymmetry, the terminal
   131	finding) which is carried forward here as context, but its *design* does not
   132	survive.
   133
   134	### The terminal finding
   135
   136	Both cross-model reviewers (Codex + Fugu) ranked above all else: high-importance
   137	abandoned memories would never be cleaned up without a hard-idle cap. This spec
   138	closes that hole structurally.
   139
   140	## Decisions (locked with owner, 2026-06-26 → 2026-06-27)
   141
   142	| # | Question | Decision |
   143	|---|---|---|
   144	| Problem framing | What to fix | (A) Unify the predicates first, then a slice of (C) redesign |
   145	| Temporal model | When does a memory decay | (3) Hybrid: recency primary (`last_accessed_at`) + absolute-idle terminal cap |
   146	| Importance role | How importance interacts | (A) Protects from normal decay, NOT from the terminal cap — no immortality |
   147	| Old spec relation | stability spec | (1) This spec supersedes it; stability returns as a follow-up |
   148	| Architecture | Multiple decay/compression lifecycle writers | (C) Single automatic decay writer; `run_salience_decay`, `memory_decay`, `memory_archive_old`, and `compress_old_memories` stop writing lifecycle; domain writers untouched |
   149	| Writer impl | How the predicate lives | (A) Canonical pure Rust fn `decide_lifecycle_state`; SQL is a permissive pre-filter only |
   150	| Importance shape | gate vs modulator | Continuous multiplier `1.0 + imp*(MAX_MULT-1.0)`, not a binary threshold |
   151	| Terminal cap | include now or defer | (i) Include now as a **dormant** composability guard-rail, proven by a parametrized test |
   152	| Config | where parameters live | Dedicated `LifecycleConfig` (NOT folded into `SalienceConfig`) |
   153	| Migration | schema change | **Zero-migration**; `SCHEMA_VERSION` stays 44; stability's migration 45 travels with the follow-up |
   154	| Advisory state | `SalienceScore.suggested_state` | Keep response shape but compute it through `decide_lifecycle_state`; no legacy predicate survives |
   155	| Execution | scheduler | No new lifecycle scheduler; existing optional compression scheduler is constrained to compression-only and cannot write lifecycle |
   156
   157	## Architecture
   158
   159	**Single automatic decay writer (C).** `run_salience_decay` stops writing
   160	`lifecycle_state` (remove `salience.rs:~439-460` state block) and becomes
   161	score-only. `memory_decay` keeps policy-score updates but stops lifecycle
   162	transitions. `memory_archive_old` stops archiving by its own age/importance/access
   163	predicate. `compress_old_memories` stops archiving originals by its own
   164	age/importance/access predicate for both retention-policy and scheduler callers.
   165	`lifecycle_run` becomes the only engine that applies decay-derived transitions.
   166
   167	**Canonical predicate (A).** Extract the decision into a pure function:
   168
   169	```rust
   170	fn decide_lifecycle_state(
   171	    memory: &Memory,
   172	    now: DateTime<Utc>,
   173	    cfg: &LifecycleConfig,
   174	) -> LifecycleState
   175	```
   176
   177	`lifecycle_run` uses SQL only as a **permissive candidate pre-filter** (cheap
   178	selection); the final decision is always this function. **Safety invariant:** the
   179	pre-filter must never be *more restrictive* than the function — it only narrows
   180	the set to inspect, never excludes a candidate the function would transition.
   181
   182	```
   183	lifecycle_run handler
   184	  ├─ SQL pre-filter (permissive: e.g. lifecycle_state='active' OR 'stale')
   185	  ├─ for each candidate: decide_lifecycle_state(m, now, cfg)
   186	  └─ apply transitions (monotonic, idempotent)
   187	```
   188
   189	## The hybrid predicate
   190
   191	```
   192	idle_days = (now − last_accessed_at.unwrap_or(created_at)).num_days()
   193	```
   194
   195	Decision order (first match wins):
   196
   197	```rust
   198	if memory.lifecycle_state == Archived { return Archived; }   // terminal, never reverts here
   199
   200	// 1. TERMINAL CAP — composability guard-rail by absolute idle time.
   201	//    Applies to ALL, including max-importance. Dormant under current defaults.
   202	if idle_days >= cfg.hard_idle_cap_days { return Archived; }   // default 365
   203
   204	// 2. NORMAL DECAY by recency — importance grants resistance (extended windows).
   205	let importance      = normalized_importance(memory.importance);                  // finite, clamped [0,1]
   206	let mult            = 1.0 + importance * (cfg.max_importance_mult - 1.0);
   207	let effective_stale = (cfg.stale_days_base   as f32 * mult) as i64;              // default 30×mult
   208	let effective_arch  = (cfg.archive_days_base as f32 * mult) as i64;              // default 90×mult
   209
   210	if idle_days >= effective_arch  { return Archived; }
   211	if idle_days >= effective_stale { return Stale;    }
   212
   213	Active
   214	```
   215
   216	`normalized_importance` is deliberately part of the predicate, not assumed from
   217	storage. Existing create/update paths accept raw `f32` importance in several
   218	places, so the lifecycle decision normalizes defensively:
   219
   220	```rust

exec
/bin/zsh -lc "rtk proxy nl -ba docs/superpowers/specs/2026-06-27-lifecycle-predicate-unification-design.md | rtk proxy sed -n '441,700p'" in /Users/ronaldo/Projects/_aiconnai/engram
 succeeded in 0ms:
   441	`archived` remains `0` in production until someone **calls** `lifecycle_run` (or a
   442	follow-up adds a lifecycle scheduler). The existing optional compression scheduler
   443	is not allowed to compensate by archiving active/stale memories; after this spec it
   444	can only compress rows whose lifecycle was already decided elsewhere. This spec
   445	fixes *what happens when lifecycle runs*, which is exactly the (A) the owner chose;
   446	"make it run" (B) is a separate follow-up, as the original option-2 framing
   447	anticipated.
   448
   449	## References
   450
   451	- Inert salience predicate: obs #14998, `salience.rs:254-278`
   452	- No lifecycle scheduler / optional compression scheduler distinction: obs #15031,
   453	  `dream/mod.rs:15`, `quality.rs:303`, `server.rs:122-136,726-749`
   454	- Feedback default 0.5: obs #15007, `salience.rs:293`
   455	- Superseded design: `2026-06-26-stability-spacing-effect-design.md`
   456	- Boundary-validation discipline carried into the renderer/handler: see the
   457	  Codex-gate lesson on validating param type, not just value.

exec
/bin/zsh -lc "rtk proxy nl -ba docs/superpowers/specs/2026-06-27-lifecycle-predicate-unification-design.md | rtk proxy sed -n '221,440p'" in /Users/ronaldo/Projects/_aiconnai/engram
 succeeded in 0ms:
   221	fn normalized_importance(value: f32) -> f32 {
   222	    if value.is_finite() {
   223	        value.clamp(0.0, 1.0)
   224	    } else {
   225	        0.5
   226	    }
   227	}
   228	```
   229
   230	### Why the cap uses idle, not creation age
   231
   232	If the cap used creation age, a memory created 400 days ago but **accessed
   233	yesterday** would archive — contradicting the chosen recency model (3). The cap
   234	is "abandoned too long" (`idle_days >= 365`), not "old". Importance protects from
   235	*normal* decay (up to 360 days at max), but genuine abandonment for a full year
   236	is not survivable.
   237
   238	### The terminal hole is closed by normal decay, not the cap
   239
   240	With `archive_base=90 × MAX_MULT=4.0 = 360d`, a max-importance memory archives at
   241	360 days idle **by the normal rule**. The terminal finding (high-importance never
   242	archives) is closed by the **continuous modulation**, not by the cap.
   243
   244	### The cap is a dormant composability guard-rail
   245
   246	Under current defaults the cap (365) is **redundant** for importance-only memories
   247	(normal archive maxes at 360 < 365). It is included now as a **safety invariant**,
   248	not an optimization: it guarantees that when `stability` (the follow-up) adds a
   249	*second* multiplier on top of importance, the effective window cannot exceed an
   250	absolute ceiling and recreate "immortal by accident" (the exact failure the old
   251	spec's 4.0 ceiling tried to avoid). The spec documents it as dormant under
   252	defaults and **proves it dominates** when configured below the effective archive
   253	window (test below). "Dormant" must be distinguished from "dead" — the inert
   254	`suggest_lifecycle_state` is the cautionary precedent.
   255
   256	## Data model & config
   257
   258	**Zero-migration.** The predicate uses existing columns: `last_accessed_at`,
   259	`created_at`, `importance`, `lifecycle_state`. No new column. `SCHEMA_VERSION`
   260	stays **44**. The `stability` column + migration 45 belong to the follow-up.
   261
   262	**Dedicated `LifecycleConfig`** (NOT folded into `SalienceConfig`, because the
   263	architectural decision is to *separate* salience-scoring from lifecycle):
   264
   265	```rust
   266	pub struct LifecycleConfig {
   267	    pub stale_days_base: i64,      // default 30
   268	    pub archive_days_base: i64,    // default 90
   269	    pub hard_idle_cap_days: i64,   // default 365 (dormant under defaults)
   270	    pub max_importance_mult: f32,  // default 4.0
   271	}
   272	```
   273
   274	`SalienceConfig` keeps only weights / half-life / frequency / salience-history.
   275	MCP-parameter compatibility (`stale_days`/`archive_days`) is handled by
   276	translating at the handler boundary into `LifecycleConfig`, without mixing the
   277	internal structs.
   278
   279	**Behavior migration (not schema):** `salience_decay_run`, `memory_decay`,
   280	`memory_archive_old`, and `compress_old_memories` stop transitioning state. This
   281	is documented in contract docs/tests; it is a behavior change, not a schema
   282	migration.
   283
   284	**Public contract migration (docs/registry, not schema):** every public surface
   285	whose advertised behavior changes must be updated in the implementation plan:
   286	`docs/MCP_TOOLS.md`, `src/mcp/tools/registry.rs`, and the mirrored tool metadata in
   287	`src/mcp/tools/memory.rs` must stop claiming that `salience_decay_run`,
   288	`memory_decay`, or `memory_archive_old` perform lifecycle transitions. The
   289	`lifecycle_run.min_importance` parameter must stop being advertised as a selection
   290	filter; for backward compatibility the handler may accept it as a deprecated/no-op
   291	or translate it only into reporting, but it must not exclude candidates from the
   292	canonical predicate.
   293
   294	## Invariants (canonical — go to the gate)
   295
   296	1. **Single automatic decay/lifecycle-policy writer.** Only `lifecycle_run`
   297	   applies automatic transitions derived from `decide_lifecycle_state`.
   298	   `run_salience_decay` is score-only; `memory_decay` is policy-score-only;
   299	   `memory_archive_old` is not allowed to transition lifecycle by age/importance/
   300	   access-count; `compress_old_memories` is not allowed to transition lifecycle
   301	   for either retention-policy or compression-scheduler callers. Explicit /
   302	   manual-domain writes (`memory_set_lifecycle`, consolidation, retention
   303	   max-count, conflict resolution, confirmed expiration at `dream.rs:377`) are
   304	   **not** concurrent decay engines and coexist legitimately.
   305	2. **Monotonicity without automatic reversal.** `decide_lifecycle_state` only
   306	   advances: `active → stale → archived` or `active → archived` directly. Never
   307	   `archived → *`, never `stale → active` automatically. Direct `active →
   308	   archived` is permitted when the archive predicate is already satisfied
   309	   (avoids run-frequency dependence — no `lifecycle_changed_at` needed).
   310	   Reactivation is explicit-only (out of scope).
   311	3. **Idempotence under repetition.** Running `lifecycle_run` N times ≡ running
   312	   once (consequence of monotonicity + pure function).
   313	4. **Permissive pre-filter.** The SQL selection never excludes a candidate that
   314	   `decide_lifecycle_state` would transition.
   315	5. **Cap archives, never directly deletes.** *This spec's* cap never deletes —
   316	   it only transitions to `Archived`. The preexisting `retention_policy_apply`
   317	   auto-delete (`retention.rs:204`) is a separate, explicitly-configured
   318	   retention domain. Its current semantics are **creation-age based**, not
   319	   "time since archived": a newly archived 400-day-old memory can be soft-deleted
   320	   by a later retention-policy apply if `auto_delete_after_days` is configured.
   321	   This spec accepts and documents that preexisting behavior to preserve
   322	   zero-migration; changing it requires a follow-up with `lifecycle_changed_at`
   323	   or equivalent archival timestamp.
   324
   325	## Test strategy
   326
   327	**Principle:** every tested gate must prove it fires AND that it does not — the
   328	inert `suggest_lifecycle_state` and the dormant cap share the failure mode that a
   329	predicate never forced to fire can be dead without signal.
   330
   331	### Pure-function tests (`decide_lifecycle_state`, no I/O), parametrized
   332
   333	| Case | importance | idle_days | config | Expected | Proves |
   334	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
   335	| Fresh default | 0.5 | 5 | default | `Active` | nothing decays early |
   336	| Stale by idle | 0.0 | 35 | default | `Stale` | stale gate fires (base 30) |
   337	| Archive by idle | 0.0 | 95 | default | `Archived` | archive gate fires (base 90) |
   338	| Importance protects from archive | 1.0 | 200 | default | `Stale` | mult 4.0 → eff_stale=120 (≥, so Stale), eff_arch=360 (not yet) |
   339	| Calibration boundary − | 1.0 | 359 | default | `Stale` | regression-lock the numbers |
   340	| Calibration boundary + | 1.0 | 360 | default | `Archived` | terminal hole closed by normal rule |
   341	| **Cap FORCED** | 1.0 | 320 | `cap=300` | `Archived` | **cap dominates** when effective_arch=360 > cap=300, idle≥cap |
   342	| Stale==threshold | 0.0 | 30 | default | `Stale` | exact boundary fires |
   343	| Archive==threshold | 0.0 | 90 | default | `Archived` | exact boundary fires |
   344	| Cap==threshold | 1.0 | 300 | `cap=300` | `Archived` | exact cap fires |
   345	| One day before | 0.0 | 29 | default | `Active` | −1 does not fire |
   346	| Importance > 1 clamps | 2.0 | 359 | default | `Stale` | max multiplier stays 4.0 |
   347	| Importance < 0 clamps | -1.0 | 35 | default | `Stale` | min multiplier stays 1.0 |
   348	| Importance NaN defaults | NaN | 95 | default | `Stale` | non-finite input cannot poison multiplier |
   349	| Already archived | any | any | default | `Archived` | terminal no-revert |
   350	| No last_accessed | 0.0 | (via created_at) | default | per age | `unwrap_or(created_at)` |
   351
   352	### Integration tests (`lifecycle_run`, real SQLite)
   353
   354	1. **Pre-filter excludes nothing valid (regression of the original bug):** a
   355	   memory with `importance=1.0` AND high `access_count` AND idle ≥ archive must
   356	   still be archived. If the SQL keeps any restrictive `importance`/`access_count`
   357	   filter, this fails. *(This is the test that proves the original
   358	   `importance < X AND access_count < 5` gate is gone.)*
   359	2. **End-to-end transitions:** populate varied idle ages, run `lifecycle_run`,
   360	   assert resulting `stale`/`archived` counts. *(The test that, if it had
   361	   existed, would have caught `archived=0`.)*
   362	3. **Idempotence:** run `lifecycle_run` twice; final state == running once.
   363	4. **Dry-run parity:** `lifecycle_run(dry_run=true)` reports exactly the
   364	   candidates that `dry_run=false` would apply.
   365	5. **Behavior regression:** `salience_decay_run` does NOT change
   366	   `lifecycle_state` (only score). Proves writer C was disarmed.
   367	6. **Behavior regression:** `memory_decay(dry_run=false)` updates policy scores
   368	   but does NOT change `lifecycle_state`. Proves the retention-score writer was
   369	   disarmed without removing the policy-decay surface.
   370	7. **Behavior regression:** `memory_archive_old(dry_run=false)` no longer changes
   371	   `lifecycle_state` through its age/importance/access predicate. If compression
   372	   remains, it only operates on already-Archived rows or is deferred to a
   373	   follow-up compression tool.
   374	8. **Behavior regression:** `compress_old_memories` no longer changes
   375	   `lifecycle_state` through its age/importance/access predicate, regardless of
   376	   entry point (`retention_policy_apply` compression or `ENGRAM_COMPRESSION_INTERVAL`
   377	   scheduler). It may create summaries only for rows already `Archived`, and its
   378	   result/log wording must count compression rather than lifecycle archival.
   379	9. **Advisory-state parity:** `salience_get`/`SalienceScore.suggested_state`
   380	   matches `decide_lifecycle_state` for the same memory/config. The legacy
   381	   `suggest_lifecycle_state` predicate cannot survive as a public second answer.
   382	10. **Retention auto-delete boundary:** lifecycle cap/run alone never soft-deletes.
   383	   A separate `retention_policy_apply` with `auto_delete_after_days` may soft-delete
   384	   already-Archived rows by `created_at`; the test documents the preexisting
   385	   explicit-policy boundary rather than silently relying on it.
   386
   387	### Out of scope for tests (YAGNI)
   388
   389	- stability (not in this spec)
   390	- lifecycle scheduler (this spec adds none; compression scheduler mechanics are covered only through its called function)
   391	- deletion by this predicate (cap only archives; retention auto-delete is separate domain)
   392
   393	## What is removed (not only added)
   394
   395	- The `lifecycle_state` write block in `run_salience_decay` (`salience.rs:~439-460`).
   396	- The divergent SQL predicate in `lifecycle_run`
   397	  (`importance < X AND access_count < 5 AND created_at`), replaced by a permissive
   398	  pre-filter + `decide_lifecycle_state`.
   399	- The `memory_decay` lifecycle transition (`memory_policy.rs:352-357`): keeps
   400	  writing policy scores, stops transitioning `lifecycle_state`.
   401	- The `memory_archive_old` lifecycle transition (`summarize.rs:231-269,329`):
   402	  stops using its own age/importance/access-count predicate to archive memories.
   403	  Compression of already-Archived rows is allowed; deciding a memory *becomes*
   404	  archived remains `lifecycle_run`'s job.
   405	- The `compress_old_memories` lifecycle transition (`retention.rs:237-312`), for
   406	  both `retention_policy_apply` (`retention.rs:157`) and the optional server
   407	  compression scheduler (`server.rs:122-136,726-749`): stops selecting active
   408	  memories by age/importance/access-count and setting them to `Archived`.
   409	  Compression may operate only on rows already `Archived` unless a follow-up
   410	  redesigns the compression surface.
   411	- **The legacy `suggest_lifecycle_state` predicate, made non-divergent.**
   412	  `SalienceScore.suggested_state` (`salience.rs:80,211,220`, exposed via
   413	  `salience_get`/`salience_score`) currently calls `suggest_lifecycle_state` —
   414	  a *third public* predicate that diverges from the canonical one. This spec
   415	  chooses compatibility: keep `suggested_state`, but compute it via
   416	  `decide_lifecycle_state(memory, now, &LifecycleConfig)` so there is exactly one
   417	  predicate behind every surface. The `suggest_lifecycle_state` fn and its tests
   418	  (`salience.rs:254-278,971-981`) are removed or rewritten accordingly.
   419	- Public MCP contract metadata that advertises old lifecycle side effects:
   420	  `docs/MCP_TOOLS.md`, `src/mcp/tools/registry.rs`, and
   421	  `src/mcp/tools/memory.rs` must be updated for `memory_decay`,
   422	  `memory_archive_old`, `lifecycle_run.min_importance`, and
   423	  `salience_decay_run`.
   424	- The old stability spec is marked **superseded** with a pointer to this one.
   425
   426	## Out of scope (explicit, with destination)
   427
   428	| Out of scope | Destination |
   429	|---|---|
   430	| `stability` column + migration 45 | stability follow-up (on this foundation) |
   431	| Automatic lifecycle scheduler | execution follow-up (the "B"); this spec adds none and constrains the existing compression scheduler not to write lifecycle |
   432	| Permanent deletion by timer (in *this* predicate) | never; the cap only archives. Preexisting `retention_policy_apply` auto-delete remains creation-age based and separate; lifecycle-age semantics require follow-up migration |
   433	| Retention-score as lifecycle input | mandatory follow-up if product wants reinforcement/durability/centrality to influence `decide_lifecycle_state` |
   434	| Redesigning compression UX/API beyond removing lifecycle writes | follow-up if `memory_archive_old`/`compress_old_memories` need a replacement compression surface |
   435	| Reactivation by access (`stale → active`) | separate product decision; this spec is decay-forward only |
   436	| Graph/coactivation as a signal | phase 2 of the old spec, already deferred |
   437
   438	## Honest consequence
   439
   440	After this spec, Engram has a **correct, unified** predicate, but lifecycle-driven

