REVIEW_VERDICT: PASS scheduler/compression blocker is fixed; safe for implementation plan with one metadata cleanup gap

- [MED] Public contract cleanup misses `lifecycle_config`. Spec defines `LifecycleConfig` without importance/access thresholds at `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-06-27-lifecycle-predicate-unification-design.md:262` and only calls out `lifecycle_run.min_importance` cleanup at `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-06-27-lifecycle-predicate-unification-design.md:284`, but public surfaces still expose `min_importance`/`min_access_count`: `src/mcp/handlers/lifecycle.rs:270`, `src/mcp/tools/registry.rs:1259`, `src/mcp/tools/memory.rs:1112`, `docs/MCP_TOOLS.md:1354`. Add this to the implementation plan as removal or deprecated/no-op metadata. Not a blocker because it does not hide another lifecycle writer.

- [LOW] Required writer inventory verified; no remaining unclassified `lifecycle_state` writer found:
  - `src/mcp/handlers/lifecycle.rs:73` / `src/mcp/handlers/lifecycle.rs:177` / `src/mcp/handlers/lifecycle.rs:184` → decay engine `lifecycle_run` → agrees with spec.
  - `src/mcp/handlers/quality.rs:303` → `src/intelligence/salience.rs:450` → decay engine `salience_decay_run` → agrees with spec.
  - `src/mcp/handlers/memory_policy.rs:100` / `src/mcp/handlers/memory_policy.rs:143` / `src/mcp/handlers/memory_policy.rs:352` → policy decay writer → agrees with spec.
  - `src/mcp/handlers/summarize.rs:225` / `src/mcp/handlers/summarize.rs:329` → `memory_archive_old` compression/decay writer → agrees with spec.
  - `src/storage/queries/retention.rs:157` / `src/storage/queries/retention.rs:217` / `src/storage/queries/retention.rs:312` plus scheduler entry `src/bin/server.rs:122` / `src/bin/server.rs:726` / `src/bin/server.rs:747` → retention compression + optional scheduler writer → agrees with spec and fixes the v2 blocker.
  - Preserved domain writers: `src/intelligence/consolidation_offline.rs:568`, `src/storage/queries/retention.rs:182`, `src/storage/queries/retention.rs:202`, `src/intelligence/context_quality.rs:730`, `src/intelligence/context_quality.rs:737`, `src/mcp/handlers/dream.rs:377`, `src/mcp/handlers/lifecycle.rs:239` → agree with spec.
  - Helpers/initializers/tests/non-lifecycle visibility: `src/storage/queries/lifecycle.rs:28`, `src/storage/migrations.rs:926`, `src/storage/turso_backend.rs:254`, `src/storage/turso_backend.rs:651`, `src/storage/queries/tests.rs:1543`, plus memory-type/generic soft-delete paths like `src/intelligence/memory_update.rs:650`, `src/intelligence/gardening.rs:297`, `src/storage/queries/core.rs:1458`, `src/storage/queries/core.rs:2033` → not decay engines.

- [LOW] Compression decision is sound. Allowing explicit retention compression or the optional scheduler to call `decide_lifecycle_state` and archive would preserve canonical predicate logic but still create additional lifecycle writer entry points, violating the spec’s single-writer invariant. The “compress only already Archived rows or split/redesign later” rule at `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-06-27-lifecycle-predicate-unification-design.md:112` is precise enough to implement.

- [LOW] Other checked decisions are safe: `memory_archive_old` must not delegate lifecycle writes without becoming a second writer; `memory_decay` can defer retention-score input because retention scoring is a separate model; creation-age retention auto-delete is explicitly documented at `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-06-27-lifecycle-predicate-unification-design.md:315`; `SalienceScore.suggested_state` unification and importance normalization are testable at `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-06-27-lifecycle-predicate-unification-design.md:216` and `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-06-27-lifecycle-predicate-unification-design.md:411`; zero-migration holds if those boundaries remain accepted.


